WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.315 --> 00:00:01.845
Alright, what is the difference

2
00:00:01.845 --> 00:00:03.525
between conditional and causal reasoning?

3
00:00:03.755 --> 00:00:06.725
This is something that often I feel like is more confusing

4
00:00:06.725 --> 00:00:08.685
than it really has to be.

5
00:00:09.105 --> 00:00:10.325
Um, because very often

6
00:00:10.355 --> 00:00:12.685
what will happen is people will confuse, uh,

7
00:00:12.745 --> 00:00:14.925
the sufficient condition of a conditional statement

8
00:00:14.985 --> 00:00:17.885
for a cause and the necessary condition for an effect,

9
00:00:18.225 --> 00:00:20.205
but actually conditionality

10
00:00:20.205 --> 00:00:22.445
and causality are very, very different.

11
00:00:22.505 --> 00:00:25.365
And so let me give you just one illustrative example to kind

12
00:00:25.365 --> 00:00:28.165
of show you why sufficient conditions are not the

13
00:00:28.165 --> 00:00:29.205
same things as causes.

14
00:00:29.425 --> 00:00:31.125
So if I gave you the following condition,

15
00:00:31.585 --> 00:00:33.605
if I got sweaty on my morning walk,

16
00:00:33.755 --> 00:00:36.965
then I must have gotten up early, right?

17
00:00:37.185 --> 00:00:39.645
So here the sufficient condition is I got sweaty on my

18
00:00:39.645 --> 00:00:42.925
morning walk and the necessary condition is I got up early.

19
00:00:43.345 --> 00:00:46.525
But it doesn't really make any sense to think

20
00:00:46.525 --> 00:00:50.005
that me getting sweaty on this walk caused me

21
00:00:50.025 --> 00:00:51.525
to wake up early, right?

22
00:00:51.675 --> 00:00:52.885
Like that's weird

23
00:00:52.885 --> 00:00:55.845
because also the getting sweaty on the morning walk would've

24
00:00:56.125 --> 00:00:58.205
happened after I woke up early.

25
00:00:58.345 --> 00:00:59.725
So how can something that happened

26
00:01:00.175 --> 00:01:02.645
after something else have caused the thing

27
00:01:02.645 --> 00:01:03.725
in the past, right?

28
00:01:04.025 --> 00:01:06.445
It becomes this kind of like mind twister of a situation

29
00:01:06.515 --> 00:01:07.805
that just doesn't make sense.

30
00:01:08.065 --> 00:01:11.005
And that's because conditional reasoning just describes

31
00:01:11.485 --> 00:01:13.885
ironclad correlations that go one way,

32
00:01:13.885 --> 00:01:15.845
essentially conditional reasoning.

33
00:01:15.945 --> 00:01:17.965
You just are like describing data

34
00:01:18.835 --> 00:01:22.125
that like if sweatiness occurs in this spreadsheet,

35
00:01:22.315 --> 00:01:25.325
then getting up early will always occur in this spreadsheet.

36
00:01:25.705 --> 00:01:27.205
Um, but it's not

37
00:01:27.205 --> 00:01:30.405
because the sweating caused the getting up early

38
00:01:30.405 --> 00:01:31.885
because causation means

39
00:01:31.885 --> 00:01:34.605
that you like literally made something happen.

40
00:01:34.675 --> 00:01:38.925
Like for instance, Jenny's punch caused me to fall over,

41
00:01:39.025 --> 00:01:41.965
you know, I was hit in the mouth with Jenny's punch.

42
00:01:42.475 --> 00:01:44.885
I've never been punched. I please let's, let's make

43
00:01:44.885 --> 00:01:46.005
that a lifelong remark.

44
00:01:46.345 --> 00:01:48.485
Uh, that I, I was hit,

45
00:01:48.745 --> 00:01:52.165
but Jenny's punch, I fell over as a result of the punch.

46
00:01:52.425 --> 00:01:54.925
You, you can see it's like a much more direct

47
00:01:55.005 --> 00:01:56.085
relationship there.

48
00:01:56.425 --> 00:01:58.765
Um, and so that is causation

49
00:01:58.765 --> 00:02:01.885
where there's like a very direct one thing makes another

50
00:02:01.935 --> 00:02:03.285
thing happen directly.

51
00:02:03.285 --> 00:02:05.125
And of course the cause would then have to occur

52
00:02:05.625 --> 00:02:06.965
before the effect.

53
00:02:07.265 --> 00:02:09.325
And so another way to see the differences

54
00:02:09.325 --> 00:02:10.765
between these two things is the

55
00:02:10.765 --> 00:02:12.285
indicators are very different.

56
00:02:12.345 --> 00:02:15.445
If you see conditional indicators, which you know,

57
00:02:15.505 --> 00:02:17.245
all nicely listed out for you in chapter four

58
00:02:17.245 --> 00:02:18.445
of the loophole, and I've railed about

59
00:02:18.445 --> 00:02:19.525
how you need to memorize them.

60
00:02:19.545 --> 00:02:22.925
And so hopefully you did, um, that all

61
00:02:22.925 --> 00:02:23.925
of those conditional indicators,

62
00:02:23.925 --> 00:02:25.125
they indicate conditional reasoning

63
00:02:25.125 --> 00:02:26.325
and not causal reasoning.

64
00:02:26.325 --> 00:02:28.165
And so I, if you, you know, aren't

65
00:02:28.165 --> 00:02:30.245
as interested in the real nitty gritty of the why,

66
00:02:30.565 --> 00:02:32.205
honestly just memorize the conditional indicators.

67
00:02:32.385 --> 00:02:34.605
That's conditionality memorize the causal indicators.

68
00:02:34.705 --> 00:02:36.725
That's causal reasoning. There you go.

69
00:02:36.835 --> 00:02:40.005
Done the, the conditionals don't, um, the conditionals

70
00:02:40.005 --> 00:02:41.485
and the causal indicators don't really overlap.

71
00:02:41.485 --> 00:02:43.645
And so you can use that as really like a nice cheat sheet.

72
00:02:43.905 --> 00:02:46.685
Um, another way that you can, uh,

73
00:02:46.735 --> 00:02:49.005
think about the differences between conditionality

74
00:02:49.005 --> 00:02:52.045
and causality is the differences in the way that we object

75
00:02:52.545 --> 00:02:53.805
to conditional reasoning

76
00:02:53.805 --> 00:02:56.205
and the ways that we object to causal reasoning.

77
00:02:56.205 --> 00:02:58.685
Because notice how if you wanna like negate

78
00:02:58.765 --> 00:03:00.125
A conditional claim, kind

79
00:03:00.125 --> 00:03:03.845
of the only thing you can do is go to direct like lack

80
00:03:03.845 --> 00:03:06.125
of supposed correlations in the data, right?

81
00:03:06.315 --> 00:03:07.565
That it's like, okay,

82
00:03:07.835 --> 00:03:10.285
like if I got sweaty on my morning walk,

83
00:03:10.315 --> 00:03:11.605
then I must have woken up early.

84
00:03:11.945 --> 00:03:15.885
The really only thing you can do to like attack that

85
00:03:16.105 --> 00:03:17.405
as a statement and say, oh,

86
00:03:17.675 --> 00:03:19.645
well I saw you sweating on your morning walk

87
00:03:19.665 --> 00:03:21.125
and I know you didn't wake up early.

88
00:03:21.225 --> 00:03:23.685
It is just saying they didn't actually happen, right?

89
00:03:23.945 --> 00:03:26.565
And you can imagine that like since this conditional

90
00:03:26.565 --> 00:03:29.525
relationship is really just describing like a spreadsheet

91
00:03:29.525 --> 00:03:32.085
level one way correlation, you're basically going

92
00:03:32.085 --> 00:03:34.365
and being like, oh, well there was a time in which

93
00:03:34.985 --> 00:03:37.765
we activated our if condition in this spreadsheet.

94
00:03:37.825 --> 00:03:39.685
We saw the getting sweaty on the morning walk

95
00:03:40.025 --> 00:03:42.565
and this other half of the correlation wasn't there.

96
00:03:42.865 --> 00:03:46.405
But because that's the totality of the claim is basically

97
00:03:46.405 --> 00:03:49.805
that like if this is here, then this is here correlation.

98
00:03:50.305 --> 00:03:53.205
Um, the only way to attack it is to say, oh,

99
00:03:53.205 --> 00:03:54.805
the correlation didn't hold.

100
00:03:55.185 --> 00:03:57.125
Um, but with causality, since

101
00:03:57.125 --> 00:04:00.645
that's such a more i immense robust sort of claim,

102
00:04:00.855 --> 00:04:03.085
there are all kinds of different ways to attack it

103
00:04:03.085 --> 00:04:04.805
and that's what our omitted options are, right?

104
00:04:05.105 --> 00:04:07.685
Um, like saying, oh, there's an alternate.

105
00:04:07.735 --> 00:04:08.965
Cause actually, you know,

106
00:04:08.965 --> 00:04:12.365
it really wasn't the punch making Ellen fall over.

107
00:04:12.615 --> 00:04:15.165
Ellen just fainted at the exact same

108
00:04:15.165 --> 00:04:16.325
time that she was punched.

109
00:04:16.985 --> 00:04:18.805
And so the punch did nothing.

110
00:04:18.905 --> 00:04:20.845
It was really the faint that made her fall over

111
00:04:20.845 --> 00:04:23.685
because here I'm attacking the actual causal nitty gritty

112
00:04:23.685 --> 00:04:25.965
relationship where it's like, oh, actually, you know,

113
00:04:26.065 --> 00:04:29.165
the punch wasn't the thing that made the fall happen.

114
00:04:29.435 --> 00:04:31.765
Whereas if it were a conditional,

115
00:04:31.945 --> 00:04:34.325
and it's like if Ellen gets punched, then she falls over

116
00:04:34.425 --> 00:04:36.725
and we still had the punch happening

117
00:04:36.725 --> 00:04:37.725
and the falling over happening,

118
00:04:37.755 --> 00:04:39.925
that wouldn't really attract the conditional relationship

119
00:04:39.925 --> 00:04:42.645
because conditionals, they don't have that kind of like

120
00:04:43.435 --> 00:04:45.045
exclusivity ish vibe.

121
00:04:45.185 --> 00:04:47.165
Not that every single causal relationship does either,

122
00:04:47.185 --> 00:04:48.445
but you get what I mean.

123
00:04:48.745 --> 00:04:51.285
Um, causation also really allows

124
00:04:51.305 --> 00:04:53.965
for a lower degree of correlation.

125
00:04:53.965 --> 00:04:55.485
Like for instance, you know, oh,

126
00:04:55.565 --> 00:04:57.285
a rise in cholesterol will increase our risk

127
00:04:57.285 --> 00:04:59.685
of heart disease or something that could be a causal claim,

128
00:04:59.865 --> 00:05:01.445
but that's not necessarily saying

129
00:05:01.445 --> 00:05:04.885
that like every single time somebody has high cholesterol,

130
00:05:04.885 --> 00:05:06.285
they always get heart disease.

131
00:05:06.465 --> 00:05:07.605
And that sort

132
00:05:07.605 --> 00:05:10.885
of 100% relationship is really inherent in conditional

133
00:05:10.885 --> 00:05:13.125
reasoning that it's like if this, then that,

134
00:05:13.265 --> 00:05:15.485
that's a 100% level claim.

135
00:05:15.635 --> 00:05:19.205
Whereas, oh, you know, you would increase your propensity

136
00:05:19.205 --> 00:05:22.285
to get heart disease when, um, in a case

137
00:05:22.285 --> 00:05:25.125
of increased cholesterol, well then, okay, then like

138
00:05:25.125 --> 00:05:27.365
that's allowing for exceptions, right?

139
00:05:27.625 --> 00:05:30.085
And not to say every single causal claim allows

140
00:05:30.085 --> 00:05:33.245
for exceptions, but many of them do, which is a,

141
00:05:33.525 --> 00:05:34.765
a significant difference

142
00:05:34.765 --> 00:05:36.525
between causation and conditionality.

143
00:05:36.745 --> 00:05:39.525
And so I hope that this quick primer on just a few

144
00:05:39.525 --> 00:05:41.405
of the differences was illustrative for you.

145
00:05:41.425 --> 00:05:43.205
And then you can, you know, not feel

146
00:05:43.255 --> 00:05:44.485
weird about this anymore.

147
00:05:44.635 --> 00:05:46.565
Alright, I love you. I'll see you soon.

